## Claude workflow (this project)

- Framing (new feature/project): BMAD analyst → PM → architect → UX, producing PRD → architecture → epics/stories, in that order. You validate each artifact before the next stage starts.
- Build: `bmad-build` implements a story; `bmad-code-review`, plus the built-in `/code-review` and `/security-review`, run before any merge.
- Review agents (code-reviewer, silent-failure-hunter, type-design-analyzer, pr-test-analyzer, code-simplifier) run only on explicit request, never automatically.
- Code navigation: if a `graft/` folder exists at the repo root, query it with the `graft_*` MCP tools before grep/read.
- Design: build or redesign UI in Claude Design (VS Code "/" menu → "Claude Design" — this is the machine-level authorization step), then run `/design-sync` and commit the synced tokens so cloud sessions pick them up too.
- UI content: use `frontend-content` before building a screen. Deliver the working product and its maintained spec; choose synthesis → exploration → detail, preserve context in the spec, remove redundant UI text and choose visuals by the question. Keep decision-critical information visible. Use `distill`, `clarify`, `layout` and `frontend-qa` as needed.
- New dependency or external service: before installing a package, subscribing to a paid API or writing an integration, search https://github.com/public-apis/public-apis (and the vendor's official skill/MCP) for a free or existing option. Check before adopting: still maintained, real pricing and quotas, licence, data location (GDPR).
- Project stage: read `stage` in `.claude/stack.yaml` (missing = poc). Size tests and CI to it with `tdd` and `ci-budget`: in a POC, a few fast local tests on the core logic, no CI test runner, no E2E or visual suites.
- Coding discipline: state assumptions and ask when the request has several readings; take the smallest change that fully solves it (reuse existing code, then the standard library, then an installed dependency, before writing new code); touch only what the task needs and match the surrounding style; turn the task into a check you can run; end with one line on what was skipped or not verified. Never cut validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security or accessibility.
- Small, obvious changes (typo, one-line fix, a config value): just do it — no BMAD ceremony.
- Bugs: `systematic-debugging` — root cause before any fix.
- Deploy: `infra-deploy` — checklist before shipping, incident workflow if production is down.
<!-- stack-check:end -->
